  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"rschema_0280\" consists of seven tables. The main table is \"Movie_Project\", which contains columns for Budget (as a floating-point number), Filming_Time (as a date and time), Project_ID (as an integer and serves as the primary key), and Project_Name and Project_Type (as strings).\nThe \"Director\" table contains columns for Director_ID (as an integer and serves as the primary key), Director_Name, Email, and Phone_Number (all as strings). The \"Actor\" table has columns for Actor_ID (as an integer and serves as the primary key), Actor_Name, Email, and Phone_Number (all as strings).\nThe \"Producer\" table contains columns for Producer_ID (as an integer and serves as the primary key), Producer_Name, Email, and Phone_Number (all as strings).\nThe \"Director_Participate_Project\", \"Actor_Participate_Project\", and \"Producer_Participate_Project\" tables are junction tables that link the \"Director\", \"Actor\", and \"Producer\" tables with the \"Movie_Project\" table. They contain foreign keys (Director_ID and Actor_ID, and Producer_ID) that reference the primary keys in their respective tables.\nThis schema ensures referential integrity by enforcing that the foreign keys in the junction tables match the primary keys in the related tables. It also ensures entity integrity by ensuring that each row in the \"Movie_Project\" table has a unique Project_ID. The schema also ensures domain integrity by restricting the data types of the columns and ensuring they allow null values where appropriate.",
  "gold_question": "The movie production company needs to manage multiple movie projects, and each project has multiple directors, actors, and producers. The system needs to record each project's name, type, budget, filming time, and relevant personnel information, including name, email, and phone number.",
